## Vercel

- **Preview deployments are real deployments.** Every branch gets a public URL. If
  previews point at the production database, then every unreviewed branch is a
  live path into production data. Either turn on deployment protection or give
  previews their own Supabase branch. Both is better.
- Environment variables are scoped per environment. Production secrets belong to
  production only. A preview holding a production service role key undoes every
  other control in this harness.
- Put the functions in the same region as the Supabase project. A round trip
  across continents on every query is the most common cause of a Next.js app
  feeling slow for no visible reason.
- Set `maxDuration` deliberately on anything long-running, and move genuinely
  long work off the request path entirely.
- Watch invocation counts and bandwidth as a cost surface. An unbounded ISR
  revalidation or a hot loop in a client component costs real money here.

## Security headers

Set these in `next.config` rather than per-route, so a new route cannot be born
without them: `Strict-Transport-Security`, `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`,
`Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin`, `Permissions-Policy` denying
what the app does not use, and a `Content-Security-Policy`. Start the CSP in
report-only mode, watch what breaks, then enforce.

## Cloudflare

- If Cloudflare proxies Vercel, the origin remains reachable directly at its
  `.vercel.app` address, which bypasses every WAF and rate limit rule you wrote.
  Lock the origin so it only accepts traffic from Cloudflare, otherwise the WAF
  is decorative.
- Rate limiting at the edge is the cheapest protection available for expensive
  routes, authentication endpoints, and anything that calls a paid API. Put the
  limits there in addition to application-level limits, not instead of them.
- Worker secrets go through `wrangler secret put`. Anything in `[vars]` in
  `wrangler.toml` is committed to the repository.
- R2 buckets default to private. Keep them private and serve through signed URLs
  or a Worker that checks authorization.
- Cache rules that ignore cookies will happily cache an authenticated response
  and serve it to the next visitor. Any route that varies by user must be
  excluded from edge caching explicitly.
